    "tStartMs": 566400,
    "dDurationMs": 5120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "ladder still kind of works, which makes the \npeople living there or more likely moving there  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 571520,
    "dDurationMs": 5920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "especially receptive to the idea that time for up \nor out is coming to an end. For a long time, this  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 577440,
    "dDurationMs": 4400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "was the place where smart people could come and \nearn truly life-changing amounts of money. even if  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 581840,
    "dDurationMs": 4800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "they didn't come from a wealthy background. Tech \nwas arguably even more meritocratic than finance  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 586640,
    "dDurationMs": 4800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "or big law or medicine, which all had their own \ngatekeeping mechanisms that favored people from  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 591440,
    "dDurationMs": 6000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the right schools or the right families. And a lot \nof the people who moved here had a similar plan.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 597440,
    "dDurationMs": 4720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Work hard, save aggressively, vest some shares, \nand get out before you burn out. Now, I know  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 602160,
    "dDurationMs": 4720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "this is a generalization and things have changed \nrecently, but for a long time, tech in particular  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 606880,
    "dDurationMs": 5360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "also promoted a less ostentatious lifestyle than a \nsimilar high-end career in finance or law. People  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 612240,
    "dDurationMs": 4720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "were earning genuinely ridiculous money in the \nvalley and still wearing company hoodies while  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 616960,
    "dDurationMs": 4320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "primarily getting their entertainment from going \non hikes and playing video games. Instead of  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 621280,
    "dDurationMs": 4960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "buying ludicrously expensive housing, the culture \nof Silicon Valley workers was more paired back and  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 626240,
    "dDurationMs": 4480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "focus on just watching number go up in their \nvesting and brokerage accounts. I know it's  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 630720,
    "dDurationMs": 4800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "anecdotal and I personally didn't work in tech, \nbut when I lived and worked in San Francisco,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 635520,
    "dDurationMs": 4480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "I had three roommates who did work in tech and \nwere all making more than enough money to live  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 640000,
    "dDurationMs": 4800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "an extremely comfortable lifestyle anywhere else \nin the world. But the housing situation and the  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 644800,
    "dDurationMs": 3760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "save everything you can culture in the area \nmeant that we were all living between three  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 648560,
    "dDurationMs": 4720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "bedrooms and one bathroom. And this kind of \narrangement was considered completely normal.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 653280,
    "dDurationMs": 4080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Someone spending all of their money to live in the \nvalley, even if they could comfortably afford it,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 657360,
    "dDurationMs": 5600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "would be genuinely unusual. Again, at least in \nmy own experience, they were doing this because  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 662960,
    "dDurationMs": 4880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "most of them had a plan to make money and get out. \nFinancial independence was already really popular  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 667840,
    "dDurationMs": 5120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "amongst tech workers. The idea being save enough, \ninvest everything, build a portfolio large enough  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 672960,
    "dDurationMs": 4160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "that you can live off the returns permanently, \nand then go do whatever you actually want to do  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 677120,
    "dDurationMs": 4880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "with your life. The math behind it is pretty \nsimple. A study from the 1990s found that if  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 682000,
    "dDurationMs": 5680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "you withdraw about 4% of a diversified portfolio \neach year, it should last you indefinitely. So, if  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 687680,
    "dDurationMs": 7120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "your annual expenses are $100,000, you need $2.5 \nmillion invested. At $200,000 a year, you need  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 694800,
    "dDurationMs": 6640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "$5 million. The target is 25 times your annual \nspending. Now, for most Americans, saving $2.5  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 701440,
    "dDurationMs": 6240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "million is a fantasy. The median household income \nis around $80,000. After taxes, housing, food,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 707680,
    "dDurationMs": 6640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "transportation, and the average $39,633 in student \nloan debt, there's not a lot left over. But for  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 714320,
    "dDurationMs": 4960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "a senior software engineer in San Jose making \n$400,000 a year at a company that also gives them  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 719280,
    "dDurationMs": 5520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "restricted stock units that have been appreciating \nat 30% annually, it was clearly possible. And for  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 724800,
    "dDurationMs": 4400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "a lot of people who came from humble backgrounds, \nit really was just one of the last escape hatches  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 729200,
    "dDurationMs": 4480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "left to financial security. Even for the people \nthat didn't want to go and retire on a ranch in  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 733680,
    "dDurationMs": 4800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Colorado at the age of 35, a lot of techworking \nculture is still built around getting experience  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 738480,
    "dDurationMs": 5360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and saving up what is charmingly known as you \nmoney. The idea is that once you have this,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 743840,
    "dDurationMs": 4640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "you can leave the big companies and go work for a \nstartup or start your own business without being  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 748480,
    "dDurationMs": 4800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "destitute in the very likely scenario that it \ndoesn't work out. So yeah, already before the  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 753280,
    "dDurationMs": 4960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "whole AI fear andor hype cycle came around, tech \nbros were already fully on board with the idea of  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 758240,
    "dDurationMs": 4480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "jumping social classes. Additionally, this idea \nof living off your investment to either live a  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 762720,
    "dDurationMs": 4640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "good life or double down into a startup feeds in \nreally naturally to the third reason why tech bros  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 767360,
    "dDurationMs": 5200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "have become so obsessed with this idea. It's \njust really good marketing. Sam Alman has said  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 772560,
    "dDurationMs": 5200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "publicly that AI will create the first oneperson \nbillion-dollar company. He even reportedly has  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 777760,
    "dDurationMs": 4720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "a betting pool with his CEO friends about what \nyear it will happen. Now, of course, we all know  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 782480,
    "dDurationMs": 5360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "that Sam Alman would never say something just to \nhype up his own products. But if this is possible,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 787840,
    "dDurationMs": 5840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "it is probably going to happen in the tech sector \nwhere valuations are well generous and a single  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 793680,
    "dDurationMs": 3760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "person isn't going to be constrained by their \nphysical limitations to produce tangible goods  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 797440,
    "dDurationMs": 4640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and services. Now, in response to statements like \nthis, people working in this field either don't  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 802080,
    "dDurationMs": 3680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "believe their own hype, in which case they might \nwant to make as much money as they can before  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 805760,
    "dDurationMs": 4720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the bubble implodes, or if they do believe this \nnarrative, then they think that business equity is  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 810480,
    "dDurationMs": 5280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "about to become a lot more valuable. And this is \nthe perfect time to be going out alone, of which  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 815760,
    "dDurationMs": 4880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the first step is gaining some level of financial \nindependence. No matter which way you interpret  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 820640,
    "dDurationMs": 5360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the leaders in the space, they are basically just \nsaying don't be poor or get rich and die trying,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 826000,
    "dDurationMs": 4720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "depending on what take you prefer. Either way, \nthe nerdy tech bros that we have grown up with are  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 830720,
    "dDurationMs": 4800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "kind of going through something. The culture in \nSilicon Valley has noticeably changed over recent  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 835520,
    "dDurationMs": 5520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "years to become far more serious. It's gone from \nbean bag chairs and salad bars to 80 hour weeks,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 841040,
    "dDurationMs": 5600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "biohacking, and token maxing with a sprinkle of \n10x productivity, whatever that means. The threat  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 846640,
    "dDurationMs": 4000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of being stuck in the permanent underclass, \nespecially in a time of sweeping layoffs,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 850640,
    "dDurationMs": 4960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "is just a great narrative to keep tech workers \non their hashtag grind set. So far in 2026,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 855600,
    "dDurationMs": 8080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "over 108,000 tech workers have been laid off. That \nis on top of roughly 122,000 in 2025 and 150,000  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 863680,
    "dDurationMs": 6000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "in 2024. Companies are naturally framing many \nof these cuts as AI related restructuring. But  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 869680,
    "dDurationMs": 4560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Deutsche Bank analysts wrote in January that AI \nredundancy washing will be a significant feature  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 874240,
    "dDurationMs": 5200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "in 2026, which is a polite way of saying that a \nlot of companies are just using AI as an excuse  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 879440,
    "dDurationMs": 4480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "to fire people they already wanted to get rid \nof because it sounds better in investor letters  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 883920,
    "dDurationMs": 5440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "than we massively overhired post pandemic and \nare now spending too much on capex. Entry-level  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 889360,
    "dDurationMs": 5040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "tech job postings specifically have dropped 50% \nat major tech companies compared to prepandemic  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 894400,
    "dDurationMs": 5200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "levels. and hiring of new graduates by the 15 \nlargest tech firms has fallen by more than half  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 899600,
    "dDurationMs": 4400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "according to an analysis by the SF standard. \nIf your employees genuinely believe that the  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 904000,
    "dDurationMs": 4000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "window for financial security is closing, \nthey are going to work harder, demand less,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 908000,
    "dDurationMs": 5520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and be less likely to push back on that optional \nweekend deployment. Now, I know this isn't exactly  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 913520,
    "dDurationMs": 4240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "shocking to anybody that's been watching Silicon \nValley for the past 3 years, and it's certainly  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 917760,
    "dDurationMs": 5280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "not surprising to people actually on the inside, \nbut again, the logical response to this is using  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 923040,
    "dDurationMs": 3680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the time that they do have to make sure that \nthey can escape the permanent underclass before  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 926720,
    "dDurationMs": 5360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "they get smited off the last ladder up. Outwardly, \nit's also clearly a marketing spin to make their  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 932080,
    "dDurationMs": 4720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "companies look all powerful. Who wouldn't want \nto invest in the company that's going to define  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 936800,
    "dDurationMs": 4800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "permanent halves and have nots, right? If these \ncompanies promote the idea that their technology  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 941600,
    "dDurationMs": 4480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "is going to reshape the social fabric like the \nsteam engine did during the industrial revolution,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 946080,
    "dDurationMs": 4960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "it goes a long way to rationalize their extreme \ninvestments into this technology, especially as  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 951040,
    "dDurationMs": 5360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the industry lines up to take two and a half AI \ncompanies through particularly public initial  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 956400,
    "dDurationMs": 4640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "public offerings this year. And of course, it's \nworth pointing out that if these listings go well,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 961040,
    "dDurationMs": 3440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the tech workers themselves will probably \nhave everything they need to escape the  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 964480,
    "dDurationMs": 5680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "underclass for good. So there is something of a \nperverse incentive to play along. And well, yeah,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 970160,
    "dDurationMs": 4880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "this is objectively the most sensible course of \naction for these people to be taking individually,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 975040,
    "dDurationMs": 3760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "but it's still really hard to ignore the \nirony of escaping the permanent underclass  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 978800,
    "dDurationMs": 4960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "by riding the investment returns of creating that \nunderclass in the first place. It sounds dumb,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 983760,
    "dDurationMs": 4480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "but it's also not anything new. And if you don't \nbelieve me, go watch this next video to find out  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 988240,
    "dDurationMs": 4080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "why tech bros in particular seem to never learn \nany of the lessons from the works of fiction  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 992320,
    "dDurationMs": 4720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "they hold so dear. And don't forget to like and \nsubscribe to keep on learning how money works."
    } ]
  } ]
